\title{Minimum orders within two symmetry classes}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We recover the finite comparisons in Table 5 of Goedgebeur and Jooken's
June 2024 manuscript, with one correction. We use the complete catalogues
of Poto\v cnik, Spiga and Verret as explicitly retained classification inputs.
A necessary test on one edge reduces the subsequent verification to a much
smaller collection of graphs. The recorded runs examine all 111,360 cubic
and 4,820 tetravalent catalogue entries. The minimum order for the cubic
vertex-transitive parameters $(g,\lambda)=(14,56)$ is 486, rather than the
printed 506. The other 117 entries agree. The exceptional graph is already
in the cited catalogue; no new graph or priority claim is made.
\end{abstract}

\section{What is retained from the catalogues}
All graphs here are finite, simple and connected. An $\egr(n,k,g,\lambda)$
has $n$ vertices, degree $k$, girth $g$, and exactly $\lambda>0$ unoriented
cycles of length $g$ through every edge. Write $n_{\rm vt}(k,g,\lambda)$
for its minimum order within the vertex-transitive graphs, and
$n_{\rm at}(k,g,\lambda)$ for the minimum within the arc-transitive graphs.
An arc is an ordered pair of adjacent vertices.

We retain two precise external premises: the Poto\v cnik--Spiga--Verret
catalogue contains every cubic vertex-transitive graph of order at most
1280, and their second catalogue contains every tetravalent arc-transitive
graph of order at most 640. These are completeness premises, not conclusions
of our parsers or of checking individual examples. The primary catalogue
page identifies these scopes and asks that both \cite{cubic,stabilizer} be
cited for the tetravalent list; it also credits Conder's regular-map data.
Our retained input files are the published cubic archive and the explicit
Magma file for the tetravalent list. Their exact byte counts, hashes,
primary addresses and transformations are recorded in the certificate.
We do not substitute a general edge-transitive list for either catalogue.

The cubic archive has 111,360 graph records. Its separate position file
specifies each record's order, index and byte interval. Our parser checks
all these intervals, reads every record, and checks the adjacency lists
for simplicity, symmetry and degree three. It also checks consecutive
indices at every order and the end of the input. Reading the complete
archive checks all three member CRCs. The tetravalent parser reads all
4,820 explicit graph assignments and rejects any remaining text except
the exact array declaration and whitespace. For each assignment it checks
its index, order, edge bounds and absence of duplicates. The mathematical
filter additionally checks degrees and connectedness for every input.
No Magma text or downloaded binary is executed.

\section{The finite exclusion argument}
For an edge $uv$ of a graph $X$, let $d_{uv}$ be the distance from $u$ to
$v$ in $X-uv$, with value infinity if the vertices become disconnected.
When this distance is finite, let $p_{uv}$ count its shortest paths.
We acquire the complete deleted-edge counting proof and its explicit
positive certificates from \emph{Cycle counts and explicit symmetry witnesses},
the separately reviewed F012 component.

\begin{lemma}\label{filter}
A graph $X$ is an $\egr(n,k,g,\lambda)$, with $\lambda>0$, if and only if
it is connected, simple and $k$-regular and every edge satisfies
\[
 d_{uv}=g-1,\qquad p_{uv}=\lambda.
\]
Consequently, failure of either equality for any one edge excludes $X$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Deleting $uv$ identifies its cycles with simple paths from $u$ to $v$.
A shortest such path is simple. In an edge-girth-regular graph each edge
belongs to a girth cycle, so the two equalities follow. Conversely, the
equalities give a cycle of length $g$ through each edge. Any shorter cycle
would contradict the distance equality on one of its edges. The girth is
therefore $g$, and the second equality gives exactly the required number
of cycles. Fixing the direction from $u$ to $v$ counts each unoriented
cycle through $uv$ once.
\end{proof}

The filter first uses the edge from vertex zero to its least numbered
neighbor. Breadth-first traversal in the graph with this edge deleted
computes its distance and number of shortest paths. Vertices enter a FIFO
queue at their first discovery. Every predecessor lies in the preceding
layer, so all its contributions have been counted when a vertex leaves
the queue. One may therefore stop when the terminal vertex is removed.
Path counts are capped at $10^9+1$. Every table target is at most $10^9$,
so capping preserves equality with each target and keeps additions within
the chosen integer type. An infinite distance is represented separately
and cannot match a positive target.

For a table row with proposed upper bound $U$, a graph of order greater
than $U$ is irrelevant to exclusion below $U$. Among the others, a failed
first-edge test rejects the graph by Lemma~\ref{filter}. Every survivor
receives the full test at every edge. The terminal record contains one
result for every catalogue entry, in its original order; the checker
rejects missing, duplicated or reordered records. Thus, subject to the
stated catalogue completeness, the least accepted order supplies the
required lower inequality. This argument does not require the first edge
to represent all edge orbits. It also does not infer symmetry from a
constant cycle count.

For the opposite inequality, we use the separately acquired explicit
symmetry certificates at the displayed orders. Each supplied permutation
is checked for bijectivity and adjacency preservation, and its generated
vertex or arc orbit is checked to cover the required set. These checks
need no assertion that the permutations generate the full automorphism
group. The corrected entry below has its own additional certificate.

\section{The corrected entry}
\begin{theorem}\label{correction}
One has $n_{\rm vt}(3,14,56)=486$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The cubic catalogue entry with order 486 and index 105 has 729 edges.
The literal record is retained, together with its full adjacency list.
The independent direct traversal finds 2,916 unoriented cycles of length
14 and exactly 56 through each of the 729 edges. Its girth is 14.
The incidence identity agrees: $2916\cdot14=729\cdot56$.
A separate deleted-edge breadth-first implementation verifies all edges.
Three explicitly supplied permutations preserve adjacency and have a
combined orbit of size 486 on vertices. This proves the upper inequality.
The complete catalogue filter accepts no smaller graph for these
parameters. Completeness through 1280, which exceeds 486, proves the
lower inequality.
\end{proof}

The fixed source, arXiv:2401.08271v2 dated 25 June 2024, prints 506 in
Table 5 on physical page 21. Theorem~\ref{correction} corrects that entry.
The same graph contradicts the unqualified sentence on physical page 12
asserting the absence of cubic connected vertex-transitive graphs on 486
vertices. The preceding discussion concerns different girth parameters;
we do not silently insert a missing restriction into that sentence or
claim to refute such a restricted statement. We make no assertion about
later editions or the priority of either correction.

\section{The complete comparison and verification scope}
The following table gives all 118 corrected minima. For degree three the
class is vertex-transitive; for degree four it is arc-transitive. Within
each row an entry $\lambda:n$ specifies the cycle multiplicity and minimum
order. Only the pair $56:486$ at $(k,g)=(3,14)$ differs from the fixed
source. The machine-readable table also preserves the printed value,
the actual minimum and the identities of all accepted minimum-order
catalogue records. Those identities are not a new isomorphism census.
\input{table.tex}

The filter was compared with direct enumeration of simple paths on all
164 connected regular labelled graphs of orders three through six and
on a connected cubic graph whose first edge is a bridge. The two methods
agree on all 1,291 tested edges and on all acceptance decisions. Six
malformed or truncated inputs are rejected. These bounded controls
support the implementation; the completeness argument is the written
proof together with the retained complete catalogue runs. The latter
are actual ordinary Python/C++ executions. The historical runner uses
Python assertions and is not approved for optimized execution; the new
packet checker rejects that mode explicitly. The default replay checks
all retained records and freshly verifies the exceptional graph. It does
not claim to repeat the complete catalogue runs.

This component proves minima only in the two declared symmetry classes.
It neither proves every remaining unrestricted lower bound nor counts
all unrestricted extremal graphs. It completes no whole-original
admission, new conjecture result, Lean formalization or length comparison.

